{"type":"message","id":"4c02baed","parentId":"cce8273e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:39:35.573Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_K5e6ZYwgeyZynrJ2yMg2S5my","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"south-carolina-bans-cbdc-protects-bitcoin-miners-2026-05-21\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"South Carolina bans CBDC participation and protects crypto users and Bitcoin miners\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"South Carolina crypto law\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"South Carolina Governor Signs Law Banning CBDC Payments and Protecting Bitcoin Miners\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/south-carolina-governor-signs-bill-protecting-bitcoin-miners-banning-cbdc\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368455/south-carolina-law-banning-cbdc-protecting-crypto-users-bitcoin-miners\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph says Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, making South Carolina one of the more crypto-friendly state frameworks in the US.\",\n    \"The law bars state agencies and political subdivisions from accepting or participating in CBDC payments or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\",\n    \"The bill protects self-custody rights and prohibits governments from restricting hardware wallets or self-hosted wallets.\",\n    \"Bitcoin miners in industrial zones receive protections against discriminatory zoning and licensing rules, including noise limits beyond general pollution rules.\",\n    \"Decrypt reports the bill also exempts miners, node operators, blockchain software developers, and crypto-to-crypto traders from money transmitter licensing requirements.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Cointelegraph RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina governor signs bill protecting Bitcoin miners, banning CBDC\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 11:21:32 +0000\\nDescription: Senate Bill 163 bans CBDC payments by state agencies and shields Bitcoin miners from discriminatory zoning and licensing rules.\\n\\nCointelegraph article text:\\nSouth Carolina Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, advancing one of the most crypto-friendly state-level frameworks in the country.\\nOn CBDCs, the law bars any state agency or political subdivision from accepting, requiring payment in, or participating in Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials, including any pilot programs run by federal agencies.\\nIt also protects crypto self-custody rights, preventing governments from restricting the use of hardware and self-hosted wallets while barring higher taxes on crypto transactions than comparable payments made in US dollars.\\nThe bill gives Bitcoin miners operating in industrial zones specific protections. Local governments cannot impose restrictions on mining businesses that do not apply to other industrial operations in the same area, and cannot set mining-specific noise limits beyond what general pollution rules already require.\\n\\u201cA political subdivision shall not change the zoning of a digital asset mining business without going through the proper notice and comment. A digital asset mining business may appeal a change in zoning to the proper court of jurisdiction,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nThe law also exempts several activities from money transmitter licensing requirements, including mining, node operation, blockchain software development and crypto-to-crypto trading. Mining-as-a-service and staking-as-a-service providers are excluded from securities classification.\\nOklahoma, Arkansas, Florida, Mississippi, Montana, North Dakota, Louisiana and Arizona are among the states that have passed similar pieces of crypto legislation in recent years.\\nCointelegraph meta summary:\\nSouth Carolina has passed S. Bill 163, banning state agencies from accepting CBDCs, protecting crypto self-custody rights, and exempting Bitcoin miners from money transmitter licensing requirements.\\n\\nDecrypt RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina Passes Law Banning CBDCs While Protecting Crypto Users, Bitcoin Miners - Decrypt\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 15:08:53 GMT\\nDescription: The crypto-friendly legislation was signed into law on Tuesday, granting protections to crypto users, developers, and Bitcoin miners.\\n\\nDecrypt article text:\\nIn brief\\n- Senate Bill 163 was signed into law by South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster on Tuesday.\\n- The bill effectively bans the use of CBDCs in the state and protects authorities from participating in any CBDC tests.\\n- It also allows for the use and self-custody of crypto assets in the state, including offering protections to Bitcoin miners.\\nIndividuals, businesses, and Bitcoin miners in South Carolina have earned protections for the use, custody, and mining of digital assets thanks to a new bill signed by the state\\u2019s governor Henry McMaster.\\nFirst introduced in January 2025, Bill 163, also effectively bans the state\\u2019s use of any central bank digital currency (CBDC), also known as a \\\"digital dollar,\\\" and its participation in any CBDC tests.\\n\\u201cAn individual or business shall not be prohibited, restricted, or otherwise prevented from accepting digital assets to purchase legal goods or services; or using a self-hosted wallet or hardware wallet, to maintain self-custody of digital assets,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nIt also notes that restrictions cannot be placed on digital asset mining businesses in areas that are designed for industrial use, so long as they comply with general sound pollution limits and don\\u2019t place \\u201cadditional stress on the electrical grid.\\u201d\\nThe bill\\u2019s final section indicates that a money transmitter license is not required for miners, node operators, or those developing blockchain software.\\nThe bill was passed by the Senate in April 2025 and then referred to the House Labor, Commerce and Industry Committee that May\\u2014which came back with a favorable report nearly a year later, this April. In early May, a House vote registered 110 \\u201cyeas\\u201d and 1 \\u201cnay.\\u201d\\nThe piece of crypto-friendly legislation is not the only one to hit the general assembly in the state. Last year, a South Carolina representative introduced the Strategic Digital Assets Reserve Act, also identified as House Bill 4256.\\nThat bill proposed that the state\\u2019s treasurer be allowed to allocate up to 10% of select public funds into Bitcoin, as the general assembly found \\u201cinflation has eroded the purchasing power of assets held in state funds managed by the State Treasurer, as well as state retirement funds.\\u201d\\nThe bill was referred to the Committee on Ways and Means in March 2025, but has not been further updated.\\nA representative for South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster did not immediately respond to Decrypt\\u2019s request for comment.\\n\\nCombined context from both sources:\\nSouth Carolina\\u2019s new law is being framed as a broader pro-crypto policy package rather than a narrow anti-CBDC measure. Both outlets emphasize that the statute protects self-custody, shields mining operations from local discriminatory treatment, and removes licensing friction for several categories of blockchain actors. The bill\\u2019s practical impact is that state and local agencies cannot join or require CBDC experiments, while Bitcoin miners and adjacent infrastructure providers gain legal clarity around zoning, industrial-use protections, and money transmitter exemptions. The Decrypt report adds legislative background, noting the bill\\u2019s long path through the Senate, House committee review, and final House vote. It also highlights a separate reserve proposal that would have let South Carolina allocate up to 10% of certain public funds into Bitcoin, signaling a broader policy interest in digital assets.\\n\\nRelated policy details worth preserving:\\nThe bill was first introduced in January 2025.\\nThe House vote in early May registered 110 yeas and 1 nay.\\nThe law covers self-hosted wallets, hardware wallets, mining businesses, node operators, and blockchain software developers.\\nThe state cannot participate in CBDC tests or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\\n\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779345575573}}
{"type":"message","id":"34a5ced4","parentId":"4c02baed","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:40:28.357Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_Sy6PIxdOc5qF6HcQrVgIveBZ","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-article-raw.md","content":"# South Carolina Governor Signs Law Banning CBDC Payments and Protecting Bitcoin Miners\n\nSouth Carolina has enacted a new digital asset law that blocks state participation in central bank digital currency activity while extending legal protections to crypto users, Bitcoin miners, node operators, and blockchain developers.\n\nGovernor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 on Tuesday, according to reporting from Cointelegraph and Decrypt. The measure makes South Carolina one of the latest US states to adopt a broader pro-crypto framework that goes beyond opposition to a potential digital dollar and into questions of self-custody, mining rights, and licensing treatment for blockchain-related activity.\n\n## What the South Carolina crypto law does\n\nAt the center of the law is a prohibition on central bank digital currency involvement at the state level. The statute bars state agencies and political subdivisions from accepting CBDC payments, requiring payment in a CBDC, or participating in Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials and similar pilot programs.\n\nThat means South Carolina is not merely taking a symbolic position against a future US CBDC. It is also trying to prevent practical adoption through state operations or cooperation with federal testing programs.\n\nThe law also affirms that individuals and businesses cannot be prohibited from accepting digital assets for lawful goods and services. In addition, it protects the right to hold crypto through self-hosted and hardware wallets, reinforcing self-custody as a protected activity under state law.\n\nAccording to the source reports, the measure further prevents governments from imposing higher taxes on crypto transactions than on comparable payments made in US dollars.\n\n## Protections for Bitcoin miners and crypto infrastructure\n\nA major part of Senate Bill 163 focuses on mining and blockchain infrastructure. Bitcoin miners operating in industrial zones receive specific protections against what the bill characterizes as discriminatory local treatment.\n\nLocal governments cannot impose mining-specific restrictions that do not also apply to other industrial businesses in the same area. They also cannot create special noise rules for miners beyond general pollution or sound rules that already govern industrial activity.\n\nThe measure reportedly states that a political subdivision cannot change the zoning of a digital asset mining business without proper notice and comment, and that such businesses may appeal zoning changes in court.\n\nDecrypt’s reporting adds another condition emphasized in the bill’s interpretation: mining operations in industrial areas remain protected so long as they comply with general sound pollution limits and do not place additional stress on the electrical grid.\n\nTaken together, these provisions appear designed to give mining businesses more certainty around site selection, permitting, and long-term operations, especially in places where local opposition has often centered on noise complaints or special-use restrictions.\n\n## Licensing relief for miners, node operators, and developers\n\nThe new South Carolina crypto law also narrows the situations in which blockchain activity can be treated as money transmission.\n\nBased on the reports, the law exempts several activities from money transmitter licensing requirements, including:\n\n- digital asset mining\n- node operation\n- blockchain software development\n- crypto-to-crypto trading\n\nThat could reduce compliance burdens for companies and individuals building or supporting blockchain networks in the state.\n\nCointelegraph also reported that mining-as-a-service and staking-as-a-service providers are excluded from securities classification under the bill, another provision aimed at giving digital asset businesses more legal clarity.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe significance of the law is that it packages several crypto policy priorities into one statute.\n\nRather than only rejecting a CBDC, South Carolina is also defining what kinds of digital asset activity should be allowed without extra state interference. In practice, that includes protecting self-custody, limiting local discrimination against miners, and reducing licensing friction for participants who help run or develop blockchain networks.\n\nThis approach reflects a broader trend among US states. Cointelegraph noted that Oklahoma, Arkansas, Florida, Mississippi, Montana, North Dakota, Louisiana, and Arizona are among the states that have passed similar crypto-related legislation in recent years.\n\nEven so, South Carolina’s law stands out because it combines anti-CBDC language with operational protections for users and infrastructure providers. That gives it a wider footprint than bills focused on only one issue.\n\n## How the bill moved through the legislature\n\nDecrypt reported that Senate Bill 163 was first introduced in January 2025.\n\nThe South Carolina Senate passed the bill in April 2025, after which it was referred to the House Labor, Commerce and Industry Committee in May of that year. The committee later returned a favorable report in April 2026.\n\nIn early May, the bill then cleared the House with a lopsided 110-1 vote before heading to Governor McMaster for signature.\n\nThat legislative path suggests the measure developed broad support over time, especially as digital asset policy became more prominent at the state level.\n\n## A wider state interest in Bitcoin policy\n\nDecrypt also pointed to another crypto-related proposal in South Carolina: the Strategic Digital Assets Reserve Act, also known as House Bill 4256.\n\nThat proposal would have allowed the state treasurer to allocate up to 10% of certain public funds into Bitcoin. The bill argued that inflation had eroded the purchasing power of assets held in state-managed funds and retirement-related accounts.\n\nThe reserve proposal was referred to the Committee on Ways and Means in March 2025 and had not seen further updates, according to Decrypt. While it remains separate from Senate Bill 163, its existence suggests South Carolina lawmakers are not only thinking about crypto in regulatory terms, but also as a possible treasury and reserve asset issue.\n\n## What comes next\n\nThe immediate effect of the law is straightforward: South Carolina agencies and local subdivisions cannot participate in CBDC payment activity or federal digital dollar pilot efforts, while crypto users and Bitcoin miners gain stronger state-level protections.\n\nThe longer-term question is how much practical business activity the law attracts.\n\nFor miners, developers, and infrastructure operators, legal clarity often matters as much as tax treatment. A state that clearly protects self-custody, limits aggressive zoning tactics, and narrows licensing requirements may become more attractive than a state where those issues remain unresolved.\n\nAt the same time, the law does not eliminate every operational constraint. Mining businesses still have to comply with general industrial rules, sound limits, and grid-related considerations. But the state has now signaled that crypto-specific restrictions will face a higher bar.\n\nFor the broader US policy debate, South Carolina’s move adds to the growing split between jurisdictions that want to encourage open blockchain use and those taking a more restrictive approach. It also reinforces how much digital asset policy in the United States is now being shaped at the state level, especially while federal rules remain fragmented.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nSouth Carolina’s new law is not just a rejection of CBDCs. It is a broader statement about how the state wants crypto to function within its borders.\n\nBy banning state participation in CBDC payments and trials, protecting self-custody, shielding Bitcoin miners from discriminatory local rules, and exempting core blockchain activities from money transmitter licensing, Senate Bill 163 creates a more defined legal framework for digital asset activity in the state.\n\nWhether other states follow with similar multi-part laws, South Carolina has now placed itself firmly in the camp of jurisdictions trying to make room for crypto users, builders, and miners while drawing a hard line against a government-backed digital dollar.\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1794,"output":1672,"cacheRead":5760,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":9226,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779345575573,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DhrM8d7dbknapDrGnDVxehvvS3RZj"}}
